{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghW9Zddy174GzQskcYwC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I couldn't find any values of Dq and B that reproduce all three bands, so I'm not giving numbers for either.\n\n**Assignment.** High-spin tetrahedral d⁷ behaves like octahedral d³ (the hole formalism with the sign of Dq reversed). The ground term is ⁴A₂, and the three spin-allowed bands are:\n- ν₁ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₂ = 10Dq\n- ν₂ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(F) = 7.5B + 15Dq − ½√(225B² − 180B·Dq + 400Dq²)\n- ν₃ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(P) = 7.5B + 15Dq + ½√(225B² − 180B·Dq + 400Dq²)\n\n**Testing the data against the model**\n1. **ν₁ fixes Dq.** Dq = 4330/10 = 433 cm⁻¹. The sum rule ν₂ + ν₃ = 15B + 30Dq then gives B = 878 cm⁻¹. But these values predict ν₂ ≈ 6 370 and ν₃ ≈ 19 790 cm⁻¹, against the observed 7 520 and 18 640 cm⁻¹. With Dq = 433, ν₂ can never exceed 18Dq ≈ 7 790 cm⁻¹. It only gets near 7 520 cm⁻¹ if B is unphysically large, and then ν₃ moves far above 18 640 cm⁻¹.\n2. **ν₂ and ν₃ alone.** The sum gives B = 1744 − 2Dq. Setting the gap equal to the square-root term, ν₃ − ν₂ = 11 120 cm⁻¹, gives Dq² − 1134.6Dq + 337 766 = 0. Its discriminant is negative, so no real Dq exists. The gap between the two ⁴T₁ bands is too small for their sum.\n3. **Least-squares fit of all three bands.** The best compromise is Dq ≈ 484 and B ≈ 728 cm⁻¹. It still misses the bands by about 510, 920 and 190 cm⁻¹, which is far outside normal assignment error.\n\n**Conclusion.** The three bands are inconsistent with the two-parameter (Dq, B) model for tetrahedral d⁷. Possible causes include a wrong assignment (for example, a spin-forbidden band or a spin–orbit component), a distortion from Td symmetry, or effects outside this model. C can't be found from spin-allowed quartet bands at all.\n\n{\"Dq\": null, \"B\": null, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": true, \"reason\": \"No real Dq and B fit all three bands in the Td d7 (⁴A₂ ground-state equivalent) model.\"}"}],"